const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why do I need all three of SPF, DKIM, and DMARC instead of just one?',
    a: 'Each protocol solves a different vulnerability that the other two cannot fix alone: SPF authorizes outbound server IP addresses on the hidden Return-Path (but breaks when an email is forwarded); DKIM attaches a cryptographic signature that survives forwarding (but does not prevent spoofing of the visible From: address); and DMARC ties both together by requiring the From: address to align with SPF or DKIM and telling receivers to block unauthenticated mail.',
  },
  {
    q: 'In what order should I set up email authentication on a new domain?',
    a: 'Always deploy email authentication from the bottom of the stack upward: first, publish SPF and DKIM for your inbox provider and ESPs and wait 48 hours for DNS propagation; second, publish DMARC at p=none with a rua= reporting address to audit your traffic; third, advance DMARC to p=quarantine and p=reject; and finally, add MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, and BIMI.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does passing SPF, DKIM, and DMARC guarantee my emails will land in the primary inbox?',
    a: 'No, in practice email authentication proves identity, not content quality. Passing SPF, DKIM, and DMARC is a mandatory ticket to enter the inbox at Gmail, Yahoo, and Microsoft, but if your domain sends unsolicited cold spam, triggers spam complaint rates above 0.10%, or lands on a DNSBL blocklist, mailbox providers will still route your authenticated emails to the spam folder (explored in our Email Deliverability hub).',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I authenticate 5 different email tools (Google Workspace, SendGrid, Mailchimp, HubSpot, Zendesk) on one domain?',
    a: 'In real-world multi-sender setups, remember the cardinality rule for each protocol: your domain publishes only ONE root SPF record (merging all 5 tools into a single v=spf1 string, or using CNAME Return-Path subdomains to save lookups) and only ONE _dmarc record, while publishing 5 separate DKIM records (because each service gets its own unique <selector>._domainkey subdomain).',
  },
];

