  name: 'Email Authentication: The Complete Guide to SPF, DKIM, DMARC, BIMI, and MTA-STS',
  url: 'https://promptdns.com/email-authentication/',
  description: 'Master hub for email authentication: complete guides for SPF (v=spf1), 2048-bit DKIM, DMARC enforcement (p=reject), BIMI, MTA-STS, and 44 ESP setup playbooks.',
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Email Authentication', item: 'https://promptdns.com/email-authentication/' },
  ],
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: f.a,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Email Authentication: The Complete Guide to SPF, DKIM, DMARC, BIMI, and MTA-STS"
  description="Master hub for email authentication: complete guides for SPF (v=spf1), 2048-bit DKIM, DMARC enforcement (p=reject), BIMI, MTA-STS, and 44 ESP setup playbooks."
  schema={[collectionSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent transition-colors">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mx-2">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim" aria-current="page">Email Authentication</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
      Email Authentication: The Complete Guide to SPF, DKIM, DMARC, BIMI, and MTA-STS
    </h1>

    <div class="pd-answer mt-6" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
        Email authentication is <strong class="text-ink">a stack of DNS-based security standards (SPF, DKIM, DMARC, ARC, BIMI, MTA-STS, and TLS-RPT) that verify sender identity, protect message integrity, and block domain spoofing</strong>. Standard SMTP lacks built-in sender verification. The 7 protocols work together in DNS to secure inbox delivery.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#stack">Protocol Overview (Master Table)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#spf-guides">1. SPF Guides &amp; Limits</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dkim-guides">2. DKIM Guides &amp; Selectors</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dmarc-bimi-tls">3. DMARC, BIMI, MTA-STS &amp; Compliance</a></li>
        <li><a href="#esp-guides">4. Per-Service ESP Setup Playbooks (44 Platforms)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#auth-tools">5. Email Authentication Tools &amp; Checkers (14 Tools)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-16">
      <!-- Section 1: Protocol Overview -->
      <section id="stack" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          How the 7 Email Authentication Protocols Work Together (Master Reference Table)
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (<code>SMTP</code>, <code>RFC 5321</code>) was designed without built-in sender identity verification, allowing any server on the internet to claim any <code>From:</code> address. Over the past two decades, the IETF and mailbox providers standardized 7 complementary DNS-based protocols, where each layer closes a specific security gap left open by the layer beneath it. The table below compares all 7 email authentication and transport protocols by DNS record location, what each verifies, the gap it closes, and its governing specification.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="pdns-table border-collapse rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 text-left text-sm text-ink-dim">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-line bg-panel/80 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Protocol</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">DNS Record Location</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">What It Verifies</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Gap It Closes / Main Limitation</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Standard</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-line">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">1. SPF</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>@</code> (or Return-Path subdomain) <code>TXT</code></td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Whether the connecting mail server's IP is authorized to send for the envelope <code>MAIL FROM</code> (<code>Return-Path</code>) domain.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Stops unauthorized IP sending; limited to <strong class="text-ink">10 DNS lookups</strong> and breaks when email is auto-forwarded.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>RFC 7208</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">2. DKIM</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>&lt;selector&gt;._domainkey</code> <code>TXT</code> / <code>CNAME</code></td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Cryptographic RSA or Ed25519 digital signature over the message headers and body hash (<code>bh=</code>).</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Proves message integrity and <strong class="text-ink">survives email forwarding</strong>, but does not prevent spoofing of the visible <code>From:</code> header alone.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>RFC 6376</code> / <code>RFC 8301</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">3. DMARC</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>_dmarc</code> <code>TXT</code></td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Whether <code>SPF</code> or <code>DKIM</code> passes <strong class="text-ink">and aligns</strong> (<code>aspf</code>/<code>adkim</code>) with the human-visible <code>From:</code> domain.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Closes the <code>Header From</code> spoofing gap, enforces disposition (<code>none</code>, <code>quarantine</code>, <code>reject</code>), and sends daily <code>RUA</code> XML reports.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>RFC 7489</code> / <code>IETF DMARCbis</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">4. ARC</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Signed in message headers (<code>ARC-Seal</code>)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Preserves the original pre-forwarding <code>Authentication-Results</code> across intermediate mailing lists and forwarders.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Rescues legitimate forwarded mail when <code>SPF</code> breaks and mailing lists modify the body (breaking <code>DKIM</code>).</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>RFC 8617</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">5. BIMI</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>default._bimi</code> <code>TXT</code></td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Displays the sender's verified SVG brand logo and checkmark next to authenticated messages in the inbox.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Provides visual brand trust in Gmail, Apple Mail, and Yahoo; <strong class="text-ink">strictly requires DMARC at <code>p=quarantine</code> (100%) or <code>p=reject</code></strong>.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>BIMI Group Spec</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">6. MTA-STS</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>_mta-sts</code> <code>TXT</code> + HTTPS policy file</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Enforces TLS 1.2/1.3 encryption and valid certificates on inbound SMTP connections to your <code>MX</code> servers.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Closes the <code>STARTTLS</code> downgrade hole where an active network attacker strips encryption on port 25.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>RFC 8461</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">7. TLS-RPT</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>_smtp._tls</code> <code>TXT</code></td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Sends daily JSON diagnostic reports detailing succeeded and failed inbound TLS handshakes and certificate errors.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Provides visibility into silent <code>MTA-STS</code> or <code>DANE</code> encryption failures that would otherwise drop mail unseen.</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>RFC 8460</code></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Deploying the stack in the right order prevents accidental email blocking: start with <strong class="text-ink">Section 1 (SPF)</strong> and <strong class="text-ink">Section 2 (DKIM)</strong>, unify and monitor them in <strong class="text-ink">Section 3 (DMARC, MTA-STS, BIMI, and Compliance)</strong>, follow provider-specific instructions in <strong class="text-ink">Section 4 (44 ESP Playbooks)</strong>, and audit your live records in <strong class="text-ink">Section 5 (14 Free Tools)</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: SPF Guides -->
      <section id="spf-guides" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          1. SPF (Sender Policy Framework) Guides (v=spf1 and the 10-Lookup Limit)
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Thirteen guides cover every aspect of Sender Policy Framework (RFC 7208), from v=spf1 mechanisms and qualifiers (~all vs -all) to diagnosing PermError failures and staying under the strict 10-DNS-lookup limit. Select a guide below to configure or repair your SPF record:
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          {spfCards.map((card) => (
            <a
              href={card.href}
              class="group block rounded-2xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5 transition-all duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-accent/40 hover:bg-panel/80"
            >
              <span class="inline-block rounded-md border border-accent/30 bg-accent/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent">
                {card.badge}
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2.5 text-base font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent">
                {card.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
                {card.desc}
              </p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: DKIM Guides -->
      <section id="dkim-guides" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          2. DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail) Guides (Selectors, Keys, and Signing)
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Nine guides explain how asymmetric public-key cryptography signs outbound messages (DKIM-Signature), how &lt;selector&gt;._domainkey DNS records store 2048-bit RSA and Ed25519 keys, and how to fix signature verification failures in transit. Choose a DKIM guide below:
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          {dkimCards.map((card) => (
            <a
              href={card.href}
              class="group block rounded-2xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5 transition-all duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-accent/40 hover:bg-panel/80"
            >
              <span class="inline-block rounded-md border border-accent/30 bg-accent/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent">
                {card.badge}
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2.5 text-base font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent">
                {card.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
                {card.desc}
              </p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: DMARC, BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, ARC & Compliance -->
      <section id="dmarc-bimi-tls" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          3. DMARC, BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, ARC, and 2026 Compliance Guides
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Twenty-five guides cover DMARC policy enforcement (p=none to p=reject), Identifier Alignment (adkim and aspf), XML aggregate reporting, BIMI inbox logos, MTA-STS and TLS-RPT transport encryption, ARC forwarding chains, and 2026 regulatory mandates. Select a guide below:
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          {dmarcCards.map((card) => (
            <a
              href={card.href}
              class="group block rounded-2xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5 transition-all duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-accent/40 hover:bg-panel/80"
            >
              <span class="inline-block rounded-md border border-accent/30 bg-accent/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent">
                {card.badge}
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2.5 text-base font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent">
                {card.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
                {card.desc}
              </p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: ESP Setup Playbooks -->
      <section id="esp-guides" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          4. Per-Service SPF, DKIM, and DMARC Setup Playbooks (All 44 Email Platforms)
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Every Email Service Provider (ESP), CRM, helpdesk, and billing platform uses its own SPF include mechanism, Return-Path subdomain, and DKIM selector names. Select your email platform below for exact copy-paste DNS records and alignment rules:
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          {espCards.map((card) => (
            <a
              href={card.href}
              class="group block rounded-2xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5 transition-all duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-accent/40 hover:bg-panel/80"
            >
              <span class="inline-block rounded-md border border-accent/30 bg-accent/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent">
                {card.badge}
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2.5 text-base font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent">
                {card.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
                {card.desc}
              </p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: Diagnostic & Lookup Tools -->
      <section id="auth-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          5. Free Browser-Based Email Authentication Checkers, Flatteners, and Generators (14 Tools)
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Audit your live email authentication records, flatten SPF lookup trees, generate RFC-compliant TXT strings, and parse zipped DMARC XML reports in your browser with zero server logging. Select a diagnostic tool or monitoring overview below:
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          {toolCards.map((card) => (
            <a
              href={card.href}
              class="group block rounded-2xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5 transition-all duration-300 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-accent/40 hover:bg-panel/80"
            >
              <span class="inline-block rounded-md border border-accent/30 bg-accent/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-accent">
                {card.badge}
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2.5 text-base font-semibold text-ink transition-colors group-hover:text-accent">
                {card.title}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
                {card.desc}
              </p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>

        <p class="mt-8 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
          Every protocol guide, ESP playbook, and diagnostic tool in this <strong class="text-ink">Email Authentication hub</strong> works together to help you deploy <strong class="text-ink">SPF (v=spf1)</strong>, sign messages with <strong class="text-ink">2048-bit DKIM keys</strong>, enforce <strong class="text-ink">DMARC p=reject alignment</strong>, and secure inbound transport with <strong class="text-ink">MTA-STS and BIMI</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7: FAQs -->
      <section id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-3">
          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
              <span>Why do I need all three of SPF, DKIM, and DMARC instead of just one?</span>
              <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <p>Each protocol solves a different vulnerability that the other two cannot fix alone: SPF authorizes outbound server IP addresses on the hidden Return-Path (but breaks when an email is forwarded); DKIM attaches a cryptographic signature that survives forwarding (but does not prevent spoofing of the visible From: address); and DMARC ties both together by requiring the From: address to align with SPF or DKIM and telling receivers to block unauthenticated mail.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
              <span>In what order should I set up email authentication on a new domain?</span>
              <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <p>Always deploy email authentication from the bottom of the stack upward: first, publish SPF and DKIM for your inbox provider and ESPs and wait 48 hours for DNS propagation; second, publish DMARC at p=none with a rua= reporting address to audit your traffic; third, advance DMARC to p=quarantine and p=reject; and finally, add MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, and BIMI.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
              <span>Does passing SPF, DKIM, and DMARC guarantee my emails will land in the primary inbox?</span>
              <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <p>No, in practice email authentication proves identity, not content quality. Passing SPF, DKIM, and DMARC is a mandatory ticket to enter the inbox at Gmail, Yahoo, and Microsoft, but if your domain sends unsolicited cold spam, triggers spam complaint rates above 0.10%, or lands on a DNSBL blocklist, mailbox providers will still route your authenticated emails to the spam folder (explored in our <a href="/email-deliverability/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Email Deliverability</a> hub).</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
            <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
              <span>How do I authenticate 5 different email tools (Google Workspace, SendGrid, Mailchimp, HubSpot, Zendesk) on one domain?</span>
              <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
              <p>In real-world multi-sender setups, remember the cardinality rule for each protocol: your domain publishes only ONE root SPF record (merging all 5 tools into a single v=spf1 string, or using CNAME Return-Path subdomains to save lookups) and only ONE _dmarc record, while publishing 5 separate DKIM records (because each service gets its own unique &lt;selector&gt;._domainkey subdomain).</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
